WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.360
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:09.410
This is one of those simulation papers where
you can look at it for three seconds and immediately

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.110
know what it’s about.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:12.750
Let’s try that!

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:17.300
Clearly, expansion and baking is happening.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:19.660
And now, let’s look inside.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:20.770
Mmmm!

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:22.820
Yup, this is done.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:27.390
Clearly, this is a paper on simulating the
process of baking!

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:28.700
Loving the idea.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:30.910
So how comprehensive is it?

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:35.290
Well, other than these phenomena, for a proper
baking procedure, the simulator also has to

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:42.820
be able to deal with melting, solidification,
dehydration, coloring and much, much more.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:48.620
This requires developing a proper thermomechanical
model where these materials are modeled as

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:52.539
a collection of solids, water, and gas.

00:00:52.539 --> 00:00:55.940
Let’s have a look at some more results.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:01.190
And we have to stop right here, because I’d
like to tell you that the information density

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:05.219
on this deceivingly simple scene is just stunning.

00:01:05.219 --> 00:01:10.020
In the x axis, from the left to the right
we have a decreasing temperature in the oven,

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:15.060
left being the hottest, and chocolate chip
cookies above are simulated with an earlier

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.189
work from 2014.

00:01:17.189 --> 00:01:20.439
The ones in the bottom row are made with the
new technique.

00:01:20.439 --> 00:01:24.319
You can see a different kind of shape change
as we increase the temperature, discoloration

00:01:24.319 --> 00:01:28.779
if we crank the oven up even more, and…look
there!

00:01:28.779 --> 00:01:30.920
Even the chocolate chips are melting.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.709
Oh my goodness!

00:01:32.709 --> 00:01:35.479
What a paper!

00:01:35.479 --> 00:01:40.429
Talking about information density, you can
also see here how these simulated pieces of

00:01:40.429 --> 00:01:45.729
dough of different viscosities react to different
amounts of stress.

00:01:45.729 --> 00:01:50.520
Viscosity means the amount of resistance against
deformation, therefore, as we go up, you can

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:54.529
witness this kind of resistance increasing.

00:01:54.529 --> 00:01:59.380
Here you can see a cross section of the bread
which shows the amount of heat everywhere.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:04.100
This not only teaches us why crust forms on
the outside layer, but you can see how the

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:08.509
amount of heat diffuses slowly into the inside.

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:10.539
This is a maxed out paper.

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:16.640
By this, I mean the execution quality is through
the roof, and the paper is considered done

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:21.540
not when it looks alright, but when the idea
is being pushed to the limit and the work

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:25.870
is as good as it can be without trivial ways
to improve it.

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:28.549
And the results are absolute witchcraft.

00:02:28.549 --> 00:02:30.890
Huge congratulations to the authors.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:36.780
In fact, double congratulations because it
seems to me that this is only the second paper

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:42.290
of Mengyuan Ding, the lead author, and it
has been accepted to the SIGGRAPH ASIA conference,

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:46.790
which is one of the greatest achivements a
computer graphics researcher can dream of.

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:50.930
A paper of such quality for the second try.

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:52.019
Wow.

